From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2000
From: Stuart Shelton <srcshelton@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ACPI: processor: clarify ignore_ppc module parameter behaviour

Document the ignore_ppc module parameter in drivers/acpi/processor_perflib.c:
on ACPI systems where the _PPC object is not implemented or is
permanently zero, the performance-state register is left untouched.
The current implementation already drops the _PPC evaluation in that
case; this commit only adds a clarifying comment so the policy is
obvious to anyone reading the function.

Ported from srcshelton/gentoo-ebuilds:
  20060-acpi-processor-clarify-ignore-ppc-module-parameter.patch

Signed-off-by: Stuart Shelton <srcshelton@gmail.com>
[ported: applied verbatim from srcshelton/gentoo-ebuilds
  20060-acpi-processor-clarify-ignore-ppc-module-parameter.patch]
Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/acpi/processor_perflib.c b/drivers/acpi/processor_perflib.c
index fdf55c285..ec13e32e8 100644
--- a/drivers/acpi/processor_perflib.c
+++ b/drivers/acpi/processor_perflib.c
@@ -43,8 +43,8 @@
  */
 static int ignore_ppc = -1;
 module_param(ignore_ppc, int, 0644);
-MODULE_PARM_DESC(ignore_ppc, "If the frequency of your machine gets wrongly" \
-		 "limited by BIOS, this should help");
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(ignore_ppc,
+		 "Set to 1 to ignore ACPI _PPC CPU performance limits");
 
 static bool acpi_processor_ppc_in_use;
 
